.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH FSCK 1M
.SH NAME
fsck \- file system consistency check and interactive repair
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/fsck
[
.B \-y
] [
.B \-n
] [
.B \-s
] [
.B \-S
]
.I filesystem ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fsck
audits and interactively repairs inconsistent conditions for
the named
.IR filesystems .
If a file system is consistent then the number of files, number of blocks
used, and number of blocks free are reported.
If the file system is inconsistent the operator is prompted for concurrence
before each correction is attempted.
Most corrections lose data;
all losses are reported.
The default action for each correction
is to wait for the operator to respond
`yes' or `no'.
Without write permission
.I fsck
defaults to
.BR "\-n " action.
.PP
These options are recognized:
.TP
.B \-y
Assume a yes response to all questions.
.TP
.B \-n
Assume a no response to all questions.
.TP
.B \-s
Ignore the actual free list and (unconditionally) construct a new
one by rewriting the super-block of the file system.
The file system should be unmounted while this is done,
or extreme care should be taken that the system is quiescent
and that it is rebooted immediately afterwards.
This precaution is necessary so that the old, bad, in-core copy
of the superblock will not continue to be used, or written on the file system.
.IP
.B Note:
v7's
.B \-s
took an argument
.RI ( X )
describing how to interleave the new free list \-
.BR \-s3 ,
.B \-s4
or
.BI \-s c : s
\- and this port's takes none.
The interleave was a rotational optimization for a moving-head pack, described by the
.I s_m
and
.I s_n
fields of the super-block; neither field exists in this filesystem
(see
.IR fs (5)),
the disk is not such a pack, and
.IR mkfs (1M)
builds a plain descending free list.
So does this.
.TP
.B \-S
Conditionally reconstruct the free list.
This option
is like
.B \-s
except that the free list is rebuilt only
if there were no discrepancies discovered in the
file system.
It is useful for forcing free list reorganization
on uncontaminated file systems.
.B \-S
forces
.BR \-n .
.PP
.B Note:
v7's
.B \-t
option, which named a scratch file for
.IR fsck 's
tables when memory was short, is not implemented and is not needed.
The tables are allocated from the heap and sized from the super-block;
on a filesystem filling one drive they come to about eight kilobytes.
.PP
.B Note:
v7 with no
.I filesystem
argument read a default list from
.BR /etc/checklist .
There is no such file on this system and one filesystem to name, so an argument
is required.
.SH "BLOCKS ARE 1024 BYTES"
A filesystem block here is
.B 3072
bytes, and every count
.I fsck
reports of used or free space is in
.B 1024-byte
blocks \- three of them to the block, as
.IR df (1M),
.IR du (1)
and
.IR quot (1M)
also report.
Two consequences: every such number is a multiple of three, and it is
.I half
what a PDP-11 would have printed rather than a sixth, v7 having counted
512-byte blocks.
.PP
A block
.I number
is not a measurement and is not converted.
.RB `` "119 DUP I=8" ''
names block 119 of the volume, which is where
.IR dd (1)
and
.IR icheck (1M)
would look for it.
Nor is a count of occurrences: ``3 BAD BLKS IN FREE LIST'' is three entries, not
three kilobytes.
.SH "THE RAW DEVICE"
Checking the raw device is almost always faster, and on this system it is also
the only way to check a mounted filesystem, there being no
.IR ustat (2).
A transfer through
.B /dev/rmd0
is subject to four conditions
.I fsck
obeys and a v7 program knows nothing about: the buffer must begin at byte 0 of a
word, the count must be a whole number of blocks, the buffer's word address must
be a multiple of 512, and the seek offset must be block-aligned.
Every read and every write this program makes is one whole block into one of four
buffers it aligns at startup.
.PP
A repair adds a fifth: a raw
.I write
may not be sourced from the text segment.
No buffer here is.
.SH "SUPER-USER ONLY"
.B /dev/rmd0
is mode 0600, because that one node is the contents of every file on the volume.
.I Fsck
is not set-user-id and must not become so: it
.I writes
that node.
.PP
It is also read \- and repaired \- through the same node the running system is
using.  If
.I fsck
modifies a
.I mounted
filesystem it prints
.PP
.RS
***** BOOT UNIX (NO SYNC!) *****
.RE
.PP
and does not return, since the kernel's in-core super-block is now older than the
disk's and letting it be written back would undo the repair.
The machine must be halted rather than shut down.
.SH "WHAT IS CHECKED"
.PP
.ne 10
Inconsistencies checked are as follows:
.TP
1.
Blocks claimed by more than one inode or the free list.
.TP
2.
Blocks claimed by an inode or the free list outside the range of the file system.
.TP
3.
Incorrect link counts.
.TP
4.
Size checks:
.RS
Directory size not a multiple of 24 bytes.
.RE
.TP
5.
Bad inode format.
.TP
6.
Blocks not accounted for anywhere.
.TP
7.
Directory checks:
.RS
File pointing to unallocated inode.
.br
Inode number out of range.
.RE
.TP
8.
Super Block checks:
.RS
Not a filesystem (bad magic number).
.br
A geometry this system cannot read: block size, inodes per block or
addresses per inode other than its own.
.br
More blocks for inodes than there are in the file system.
.RE
.TP
9.
Bad free block list format.
.PP
.B Note:
v7 listed a tenth, ``total free block and/or free inode count incorrect'', as a
repair.  The two super-block counters it names,
.I s_tfree
and
.IR s_tinode ,
are maintained by nothing in this system \(em not by the kernel, which never
touches them \(em so they are stale on any filesystem that has been written to
and a repair would be undone by the next write.
.I Fsck
reports a discrepancy as a note, asks nothing, and leaves both fields correct
when it has had reason to rewrite the super-block anyway.
.PP
Orphaned files and directories (allocated but unreferenced) are,
with the operator's concurrence, reconnected by
placing them in the
.B lost+found
directory.
The name assigned is the inode number.
The only restriction
is that
.B lost+found
must preexist
in the root of the filesystem being checked.
.PP
.B Note:
v7 added that it must also ``have empty slots in which entries can be made'',
obtained by creating files in it and deleting them again.
That is not true here.
.I Fsck
rounds the directory's size up to a whole block before it looks, a block holds
128 entries, and everything past
.B .
and
.B ..
is zero \(em so a
.B lost+found
made by
.IR mkdir (1)
and never touched has 126 free slots.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.BI "Can't stat " filesystem
The argument does not exist.
.TP
.BI "Can't open " filesystem
It exists and could not be opened for reading.
.TP
.IB filesystem ": not a filesystem"
The super-block's magic number is wrong.  Nothing is checked.
.TP
.IB filesystem ": filesystem geometry mismatch"
It is a filesystem, made by a system whose block size, inodes per block or
addresses per inode differ from this one's.  The kernel would refuse to mount it
for the same reason; see
.IR fs (5).
.TP
.BI "Size check: fsize " n " isize " m
The super-block's two sizes are not consistent with each other.
.TP
.B Can't get memory
The tables do not fit.  On a filesystem this system can address they always do.
.TP
.BI "CAN NOT READ: BLK " n
An I/O error.  On the raw device this most often means the transfer broke one of
the conditions above rather than that the disk is bad.
.TP
.B Incompatible options: \-n and \-s
Salvaging the free list is a write.
.SH FILES
.TP
/lost+found
directory for connecting files with no directory entry
.SH "SEE ALSO"
dcheck(1M), icheck(1M), mkfs(1M), df(1M), fs(5)
.SH BUGS
Inode numbers for
.B .
and
.B ..
in each directory should be checked for validity.
.br
The
.B \-b
option of
.IR icheck (1M)
should be available.
.br
A second mounted filesystem is not recognized as mounted: only the root is, and
only because its name can be derived.  There is no
.IR ustat (2)
and, so far, nothing else to mount.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 if every filesystem named was examined, 4 if interrupted, 8 on a fatal error.
A filesystem that could not be opened or was not a filesystem is reported and
skipped; as in v7, that alone does not change the exit status.
